  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/10e-catfishes-from-asia/nemapteryx_nenga_en/",
      "html": "<p>Nemapteryx nenga is a catfish from India, formerly placed in the genus Arius. It is not a shark, despite the name \"freshwater shark\". It reaches about 30 cm and should be kept in large aquariums only.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/ariidae/kyataphisa_nenga.htm",
      "html": "<ul><li>IUCN Red List: Not Evaluated</li></ul>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/ariidae/kyataphisa_nenga.htm",
      "html": "<p>South and southeast Asia: India, Thailand, Myanmar, Malaysia and Indonesia. Type locality: Ganges River, India.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "habitat",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/10e-catfishes-from-asia/nemapteryx_nenga_en/",
      "html": "<p>These \"freshwater sharks\" live in the mouths of large rivers, so they can live in pure freshwater as well as in pure seawater.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "waterConditions",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/10e-catfishes-from-asia/nemapteryx_nenga_en/",
      "html": "<p>Keeping them in brackish water with a salt content of 5-10 grams per litre is recommended.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "behaviour",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/10e-catfishes-from-asia/nemapteryx_nenga_en/",
      "html": "<p>These catfish should always be kept in groups (5-10 specimens). Singly kept specimens do not thrive very well.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "reproduction",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/10e-catfishes-from-asia/nemapteryx_nenga_en/",
      "html": "<p>As far as is known, all species of the Arius relatives are mouthbrooders. The male takes the eggs into his mouth until they hatch.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/10e-catfishes-from-asia/nemapteryx_nenga_en/",
      "html": "<p>Because of its nicely shaped dorsal fin, the popular name \"whimple freshwater shark\" could be used.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/ariidae/kyataphisa_nenga.htm",
      "html": "<p>Description: Palatal tooth patches distinctly triangular, their long axes at about 70° to curve of mouth.</p><p>Etymology: The genus name Kyataphisa is a Bengali word meaning catfish. The specific name nenga is from Nenga gagora, the local Bengali name for this catfish in India.</p><p>Remarks: On some online searches this species can still be found as Nemapteryx nenga.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "references",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/ariidae/kyataphisa_nenga.htm",
      "html": "<p>Kailola, P. J. 2004 (Dec.) A phylogenetic exploration of the catfish family Ariidae (Otophysi: Siluriformes). The Beagle, Records of the Museums and Art Galleries of the Northern Territory v. 20: 87-166. Marceniuk, Alexandre P.; Menezes, Naércio A. (2007). \"Systematics of the family Ariidae (Ostariophysi, Siluriformes), with a redefinition of the genera\" Zootaxa 1416: 1–126. Marceniuk, A. P., C. Oliveira and C. J. Ferraris, Jr. 2023 (21 Aug.) A new classification of the family Ariidae (Osteichthyes: Ostariophysi: Siluriformes) based on combined analyses of morphological and molecular data. Zoological Journal of the Linnean Society Advance access: 1-51. The ETYFish Project Fish Name Etymology Database © Christopher Scharpf.</p>"
    }
